<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Nile</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1798">1798</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Aboukir Bay · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> was a naval battle fought on 1–3 August 1798 in Aboukir Bay, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. A British fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Horatio Nelson</a> attacked the French fleet that had carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>'s army to Egypt, destroying or capturing eleven of its thirteen ships of the line, including the flagship L'Orient, which exploded. The victory stranded the French army in Egypt and restored British naval dominance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-nile.jpg" alt="Battle of the Nile" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Arnald · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Battle_of_the_Nile.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>'s victories in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> remained the only major European power still at war with the French Republic. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-directory">French Directory</a> considered invading Britain or Ireland but found British control of northern waters an obstacle, and the French navy was dominant only in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Bonaparte proposed an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> as a first step in a campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>.</p><p>His fleet sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulon">Toulon</a> on 19 May 1798, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> from the Knights of St. John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in June, and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> sent a squadron under Rear-Admiral Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Horatio Nelson</a> to find out the purpose of the expedition and defeat it. He pursued the French for more than two months and several times missed them by hours.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Vice-Admiral François-Paul Brueys d'Aigalliers anchored his line in Aboukir Bay, a shallow anchorage about 32 km north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, and believed his position was strong. His crews were needed ashore to find provisions, so he had no scouts out. Nelson's fleet was sighted on 1 August and attacked at once. His ships split into two divisions, one passing between the French line and the shore and the other engaging its seaward side, so the leading French ships were caught in a crossfire.</p><p>The French vanguard surrendered during the evening. At 21:00 fire was seen on the lower decks of the flagship Orient, and at 22:00 it reached the magazines and the ship exploded. Brueys had been killed. Fighting resumed at dawn on 2 August, and the surviving French ships of the line gradually pulled away to the east. Only two ships of the line and two frigates escaped.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>British losses were recorded as 218 killed and about 677 wounded. French losses are harder to calculate; estimates range from 2,000 to 5,000, with a suggested median of 3,500, and about half the dead died on Orient. Nelson was wounded in the battle.</p><p>The victory reversed the strategic position in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s army trapped in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. British naval dominance off the Syrian coast contributed to the French defeat at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> in 1799, before Napoleon left Egypt. The result also encouraged other European states to turn against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and was a factor in the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-second-coalition">War of the Second Coalition</a>. Nelson was made Baron Nelson of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and Burnham Thorpe, though he was privately dissatisfied with the reward.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Nile” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The American War of Independence ended in defeat after the surrender at Yorktown in 1781, but from 1793 Britain led coalitions against revolutionary France, and Horatio Nelson destroyed a French fleet at the <b>Battle of the Nile</b> in 1798.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>He landed at Alexandria in July, defeated the Mamluk beys at the Battle of the Pyramids and entered Cairo, but Nelson destroyed his fleet at <b>Aboukir Bay</b> in August 1798.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-occupation-of-egypt">French occupation of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
